(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-1-general-microbiology-quiz"]={"title":"Review of General Microbiology — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is true of temperature as a physical control method?","choices":["High temperature can kill; low temperature will NOT — it only slows the rate of microbial growth","Both high and low temperatures kill equally well","Low temperature can kill; high temperature will NOT — it only slows the rate of microbial growth","Temperature has no reliable effect on microbial populations"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Refrigeration and freezing preserve food by slowing growth, not by sterilising it. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How does Salmonella enterica establish a niche for replication in gut cells?","choices":["By producing a capsule that prevents phagocytosis","By binding the sialic acid receptors on host cells with a surface hemagglutinin protein","By producing an endotoxin released only on cell lysis","By using a secretion system that injects effector proteins into host gut cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. That secretion system is what allows entry into cells. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What do all viruses have, and what shapes does it take?","choices":["Capsids — protein coats enclosing and protecting the nucleic acid — which may be helical, icosahedral or complex","Envelopes — lipid coats enclosing and protecting the capsid — which may be helical, icosahedral or complex","Cell walls of peptidoglycan, which may be thick or thin","Both deoxyribonucleic acid and ribonucleic acid together"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Viruses contain either deoxyribonucleic acid or ribonucleic acid, and may additionally have an envelope or spikes. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Viral alteration of host deoxyribonucleic acid may activate oncogenes. What else can do this?","choices":["Osmotic pressure and pH change","Exposure to radiation or chemicals","Conjugation and transformation","Desiccation and phagocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Radiation and chemicals activate oncogenes alongside viruses, which is what links infection to malignant transformation. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"Why do animal viruses require uncoating when phages do not?","choices":["Because animal viruses have no capsid to remove","Because animal viruses integrate into the host chromosome first, so the genetic material must be excised — driven by the difference in cytoplasmic pH","Because animal viruses replicate outside the cell","Because the entire virion is engulfed, so the genetic material must then be released — driven by the difference between cytoplasmic and extracellular pH"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A phage injects its genome through the wall instead, so nothing needs unwrapping. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"Which grouping of microbe types is correct?","choices":["Bacteria and fungi are prokaryotic; protozoa and helminths are eukaryotic; viruses are simply very small bacteria","All listed microbes are eukaryotic except viruses","Bacteria are prokaryotic; fungi, protozoa and helminths are eukaryotic; viruses are noncellular infectious agents","Bacteria and viruses are prokaryotic; all others are eukaryotic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is how the types of microbes divide. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which statement about bacterial plasmids is correct?","choices":["They are linear molecules, always integrated into the chromosome, and are essential to bacterial growth, to normal metabolism and to cell wall synthesis","They are protein structures used for motility","They are the sole site of the bacterial chromosome","Small circular double-stranded DNA, free or integrated, which may encode antibiotic resistance, tolerance to toxic metals, enzymes and toxins"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. They are duplicated and passed to offspring, and are readily manipulated for genetic engineering. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Why does a Gram-negative organism appear red at the end of the stain?","choices":["It loses crystal violet during decolorization and takes up the safranin counterstain","It never binds the crystal violet stain in the first place during the procedure","Its teichoic acid binds safranin preferentially","Its thick peptidoglycan sheath traps safranin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Retention or loss of crystal violet is what the whole technique turns on. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What are the three shapes of bacteria, and why does shape matter?","choices":["Cocci, bacilli and spiral — because shape is used in identification","Cocci, bacilli and filamentous — because shape determines virulence","Round, flat and segmented — because shape determines Gram reaction","Yeast, hyphal and pleomorphic — because shape determines motility"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Spiral covers helical, comma, twisted rod and spirochete forms. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"What is a deoxyribonucleic acid mutation?","choices":["Transfer of a plasmid from one bacterial cell to another, which may then impact the phenotype","Uptake of naked DNA from the environment","A change in the base sequence of the genome, which may then impact the phenotype of the organism","Integration of a viral genome as a prophage"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mutations occur naturally at a low level but may be induced by radiation, chemicals or viruses, and may be beneficial, neutral or deleterious. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Enriched media are defined by what?","choices":["Agents that inhibit the growth of some microbes while encouraging the growth of the desired ones on the culture plate","Complex organic substances such as blood, serum, haemoglobin or special growth factors required by fastidious microbes","The ability to display visible differences among organisms","Containing only chemically definable ingredients"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The point is supporting organisms that will not otherwise grow. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Prions are described as which of the following?","choices":["Short pieces of RNA with no protein coat","Protein coats enclosing a nucleic acid genome","Misfolded proteins containing no nucleic acid, causing spongiform encephalopathies","Tiny obligate intracellular bacteria that lack a cell wall, causing spongiform encephalopathies"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Examples given are scrapie in sheep and goats, bovine spongiform encephalopathy, and Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease in humans. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which step of the Gram stain technique is the most critical?","choices":["Flooding the slide with crystal violet for one full minute","Flooding with Gram's iodine for one minute","Flooding with safranin for one minute","Decolorizing with acetone alcohol for about ten seconds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is called the most critical step and the one most affected by technical variations in timing and reagents. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"Which three techniques fall under genetic and molecular analysis?","choices":["Gram staining, culture, and colony counting","Serology, biochemistry, and chemical analysis","Guanine plus cytosine base composition, deoxyribonucleic acid analysis using genetic probes, and nucleic acid sequencing with ribosomal RNA analysis","Colony appearance on selective agar, motility testing in semisolid medium, and spore staining with capsule staining under the light microscope"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All three sit under the genetic and molecular heading. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Decimal reduction time is defined as which of the following?","choices":["The time taken for a population to double","The time taken to kill 90% of the organisms","The time taken to kill 100% of the organisms","The time taken for the culture to enter the stationary phase"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because the death rate is constant, each successive interval removes another 90% of what remains. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What property should an antimicrobial chemotherapeutic agent have, and what does that mean?","choices":["Broad spectrum — active against every class of organism","Bactericidal action — killing rather than inhibiting","Natural origin — a substance produced by one microorganism against another","Selective toxicity — working against the microbial agent without harming the host"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Penicillin, from the Penicillium mold and active against Gram-positive bacteria, is the example given. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"What are the two basic mechanisms by which microbial control works?","choices":["Inhibition of ribosomal function, and blockade of bacterial transcription and translation","Removal of water and lowering of temperature","Prevention of adhesion and blockade of invasion","Alteration of membrane permeability, and denaturation of proteins and nucleic acids"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The types of control are then physical methods, chemical methods, and clinical antimicrobial chemotherapeutic agents. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What are the six steps of phage replication in order?","choices":["Penetration, adsorption, assembly, replication, maturation, release","Adsorption, penetration, replication, assembly, maturation, release","Adsorption, replication, penetration, maturation, assembly, release","Uncoating, adsorption, penetration, replication, assembly, release"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adsorption is binding to a specific molecule on the host cell; release lets the viruses infect other cells. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What is adsorption, in phage replication?","choices":["The completion of viral formation","The entry of the genome into the host cell","Binding of the virus to a specific molecule on the host cell","The departure of completed virions to go on and infect other cells"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Specificity at this first step is what determines host range. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"What characterises the lag phase of the bacterial growth curve?","choices":["Maximum growth, sustained while nutrients and environment remain favourable","A flat period of adjustment and enlargement, with little growth","Rate of cell growth equalling rate of cell death","Exponential death of cells in their own wastes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cells are adapting rather than dividing. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"Blood agar and mannitol salt agar are each given as examples of which media categories?","choices":["Blood agar is synthetic; mannitol salt agar is complex","Both are general purpose media","Blood agar is selective and differential; mannitol salt agar is enriched and differential","Blood agar is enriched and differential; mannitol salt agar is selective and differential"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blood agar's differential property is the gamma, beta and alpha haemolysis patterns. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Endospores are produced for what purpose, and by which genera?","choices":["As a survival mechanism, by Bacillus and Clostridium","As a reproductive mechanism, by Streptococcus and Staphylococcus","As an adhesion mechanism, by Escherichia and Salmonella","As a motility mechanism, by Mycoplasma and Chlamydia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Genetic material is surrounded by a resistant spore coat and metabolism drops to a very low level. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are the three stated functions of the glycocalyx?","choices":["Conjugation, motility, and adhesion","Storage of phosphate, oil droplets and waste products","Involvement in biofilm production, prevention of desiccation, and prevention of phagocytosis","Crosslinking peptidoglycan, retaining crystal violet, and resisting osmotic stress"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Preventing phagocytosis is called out as a factor of pathogenicity, with Streptococcus pneumoniae as the example. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"Mycoplasmas are characterised by which feature?","choices":["Transmission exclusively by ticks and lice","Tiny pleomorphic organisms that lack a cell wall","A thick peptidoglycan sheath retaining crystal violet","An outer membrane containing lipopolysaccharide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mycoplasma pneumoniae is the example given, and the absent cell wall is the defining feature. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What are virulence factors?","choices":["Host molecules that recognise conserved pathogen structures","Pathogen molecules that enhance the ability to cause disease","Environmental conditions that favour pathogen survival","Genetic sequences that confer antibiotic resistance only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The listed factors are toxins, adhesins and invasins, which facilitate colonisation, invasion and evasion of the host immune response. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"The pilus is coded by what, and used for what?","choices":["The chromosome; used for adhesion to mucosal surfaces","A plasmid; used in bacterial conjugation","A prophage; used in transduction","The spore coat; used in survival"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both halves are given explicitly, which is what links the pilus to horizontal gene transfer. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"Escherichia coli uses type I fimbriae to adhere to which receptor, facilitating colonisation of which site?","choices":["Sialic acid receptors; the respiratory tract","Complement receptors; the bloodstream","Mannose receptors; the urinary tract","Toll-like receptors; the gut epithelium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. That is the adhesion mechanism behind urinary tract colonisation. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Bacterial growth refers to an increase in what, and occurs by which process?","choices":["Increase in cell number, by binary fission","Increase in cell size, by binary fission","Increase in cell number, by conjugation","Increase in colony diameter, by sporulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Growth means number rather than size of the cell, and binary fission gives exponential rather than arithmetic growth. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which two agents are true chemical sterilants?","choices":["Chlorine and bromine, used on water and surfaces, and alcohol, used on skin, instruments and laboratory work surfaces","Lysol and Listerine","Ethylene oxide, used on plastics and spices, and beta propiolactone, used on vaccines, tissue grafts and surgical instruments","Alcohol and ultraviolet radiation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Very few true chemical sterilants exist. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What distinguishes Chlamydias from Rickettsias?","choices":["Chlamydias are NOT transmitted by arthropod vectors; Rickettsias are transmitted by fleas, ticks and lice","Chlamydias lack a cell wall; Rickettsias have one","Chlamydias are eukaryotic; Rickettsias are prokaryotic","Chlamydias are free-living organisms, whereas Rickettsias are obligate intracellular pathogens"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Both are tiny obligate intracellular pathogens; vector transmission is the stated difference. — Lecture 1  Dr. Webster -  Review of General Microbiology .pptx, Slide 15"}]};
